# Local benchmark evidence

Measured on 2026-10-15, CPython 3.14.7, Arch Linux x86_64 / kernel 7.2.4.
These are local wall times, not hosted runner measurements. The account restriction
prevents meaningful hosted step timing. The final benchmark and essential regression timing overlapped each other;
earlier measurements overlapped full certificate replay. These are observed
samples rather than controlled hardware
comparisons or statistical performance guarantees.

## Reproduce

```sh
python3 -B tools/benchmark_local_ci.py --samples 3 \
  --registry-sizes 10 100 500 --output /tmp/e99-ci-benchmarks.json
```

The script creates a disposable clone of the actual repository, commits five
synthetic PR scenarios, and runs trusted integrity checks against the full
canonical metadata in that clone. It executes the example's full 289-case finite
regression plus its negative control for each eligible package scenario. The
external-input fixture is explicitly read by the verifier. No actual research
file is changed outside the disposable clone. Synthetic commit IDs in the raw
JSON are measurement labels, not preserved research branches.

[Raw measured samples](benchmark-results.json) record the control commit, platform,
three planning samples per case, integrity time, actual replay time and workload
counts. The baseline was `806b8ad3867468587d6650bad8a6bbfe1098d9b9` with 94
registered certificates. The concurrent worker's update to `8c6f3712` was integrated
before the final benchmark without changing the registered count.

## Measured work

| Synthetic PR | Classification | Median plan (s) | Integrity (s) | Plan + integrity (s) | Scoped replay (s) | Previous certificate selection | Proposed work |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
| Existing allowlisted documentation | `fast-only` | 1.202 | 3.077 | 4.279 | — | 0 | 0 certificates, 0 packages |
| Isolated package clarification | `targeted-replay` | 1.537 | 2.475 | 4.013 | 1.197 | 94 | 0 certificates, 1 package |
| Declared dependency outside package | `targeted-replay` | 1.000 | 2.524 | 3.524 | 0.996 | 94 | 0 certificates, 1 package |
| Canonical state file | `full-verification-required` | 1.237 | 2.625 | 3.862 | — | 94 | No replay; review/full verification required |
| Unknown input | `full-verification-required` | 1.184 | 2.373 | 3.557 | — | 94 | No replay; review/full verification required |

The previous default `verify_prelaunch.py --base-ref origin/main` passed 12/12
stages in **76.296 seconds** on the initial clean worktree, before executing any
of the 94 certificate scripts. It included 133 tooling tests and 18 repository
tests plus publication/dependency inventory. This is the prior default's measured
local integrity cost, not the duration of all certificates or a hosted job.

The new workflow also runs essential trusted tooling regressions: **69 tests
passed in 14.034 seconds wall time** (13.738 seconds inside unittest). Adding
that separately measured cost to the plan/integrity phases gives **17.56–18.31
seconds** of local Tier 0 command work for these scenarios. This sum is an
estimate from separately measured phases, not a hosted end-to-end observation. Checkout, runner startup and wheel installation are excluded and remain
unmeasured on GitHub. The sub-60-second Tier 0 goal is supported for local command
work on this machine; no hosted runtime promise is made.

The example replays measured here take 1.00–1.20 seconds **including a fresh
impact recomputation**. They are small exact fixtures, not evidence that an
arbitrary scientific package finishes in two minutes. Two minutes is a hard
per-package budget; a slower package times out and fails rather than reducing its
domain. The 240-second workflow batch cap covers two serial packages.

## Registry scale, measured separately

The same real-repository clone was expanded with synthetic valid manifests. One
package README was changed; every run selected exactly one package.

| Registered packages | Median plan (s), three samples | Selected packages |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 10 | 1.144 | 1 |
| 100 | 1.229 | 1 |
| 500 | 1.666 | 1 |

These synthetic packages share verifier and input blobs; this exercises registry
metadata and selective planning, not 500 distinct large mathematical domains.
Timings vary with machine load and cache effects. Batch Git blob reads,
shared content caches and cached ancestry avoid a subprocess per manifest or
repeated ancestor probe. Registries have a 1,000-package discovery cap, 32 MiB
manifest-read budget and 64 MiB total cached input budget. Exceeding a budget
requires review rather than expanding automatic work indefinitely.

## Queue projections, not measurements

Automatic execution is currently gated off: 10, 100 and 500 simultaneous PRs
consume **zero runner jobs** under the unset switches. After owner enablement,
the workflow creates N planning jobs and at most N serial replay jobs. Per-PR
cancellation removes superseded heads, not work for distinct PRs.

For an illustrative projection only, suppose two slots are available, all work
arrives together, runner/setup overhead is zero, planning service time P=20 s,
and an ordinary package replay job R=120 s. These assumptions are deliberately
round and are **not hosted measurements**:

| PR burst N | Plans only: N×20/2 | Every PR replays: N×(20+120)/2 |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 10 | 100 s | 700 s (11.7 min) |
| 100 | 1,000 s (16.7 min) | 7,000 s (116.7 min) |
| 500 | 5,000 s (83.3 min) | 35,000 s (9.7 h) |

These are idealized work/C drain estimates. Setup, other workflows, account limits,
job ordering and running cancellations can increase latency. A two-package batch
can use 240 declared replay seconds. The new architecture prevents automatic
94-certificate amplification and unbounded matrices; it cannot remove a finite
runner fleet's queue for 500 independent PRs. At that volume use local verification
and staged owner admission until a separately reviewed global scheduler is justified.
No billing adjustment or automatic retry is part of this migration.

## Validation evidence

The [adversarial test map](ADVERSARIAL_TESTS.md) identifies the required failure
cases. Final command counts/results and the full verifier outcome are recorded
in the implementation PR. Canonical source/certificate hashes must remain equal
to the integrated base; new CI test success alone never proves those theorems.
